gearbench (1.1.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * New keyboards: the G515 TKL, which has run on the hardware, and three
    trials not yet checked there: the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL, the G915 TKL
    (G913 TKL too) and the G815 (G813 too). Each gets per-key lighting, its
    animations and brightness. The G515 TKL runs Cycle, Wave and Ripple in
    its own versions, each checked on it. The G915 TKL's F1 to F12 bind to a
    key or a macro as the G915's G keys do.
  * New mice: the G502 HERO, tried on a G502 SE HERO, whose DPI stages are
    written to its onboard profile 1 (checked on it), and seven trials not
    yet checked on the hardware: the G502 X, G502 X LIGHTSPEED, G502 X PLUS
    MILLENNIUM FALCON, G502 LIGHTSPEED, PRO X SUPERLIGHT, PRO WIRELESS and
    G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO. Gearbench reads each and sets its DPI live; in
    host mode, not yet checked on any of them, it drives their buttons and
    sets their report rate live. Nothing is written to the seven's onboard
    memory. Sync with keyboard drives each one with lighting, and a button
    can be bound to DPI cycle.
  * The Logitech G432 headset, checked once on the hardware, on a page of
    its own in the Headset tab: its sidetone; Logitech G432 Equalizer, an
    output with ten bands and two shelves, three shipped presets and up to
    16 in all, an A50 preset copied in; noise suppression for its
    microphone; and spatial sound, which now plays to either headset. The
    equalizer and noise suppression are off until switched on, and off again
    after a logout.
  * The Astro A50 keeps up to 16 EQ presets of your own, apart from the base
    station's three, and loads one into slot 1, 2 or 3, unsaved until Save.
    Each of its three presets gets Reset to default. Both were checked on
    the base station; the Load preset to headset… dialog, built after that
    check, is not yet checked on the hardware.
  * gearbench-ctl report prints what each Logitech device on USB says about
    itself, to paste into the repository's new Device report form, so a
    model Gearbench does not drive yet can be supported. It sends reads
    alone, reads no serial number and prints no computer or user name. Each
    model Gearbench has not yet run on asks for a report in status, the
    journal and its tab.
  * While two keyboards are plugged in, a switcher at the top of the
    Keyboard tab picks the one Gearbench drives and the mouse follows; the
    Mouse tab picks its mouse with a switcher too. A keyboard switched off
    is grayed out there, and the other is driven meanwhile. Each keyboard
    model keeps its own lighting and brightness, and each mouse model its
    own button bindings. A keyboard is offered only the effects it reports,
    and Wave runs in six directions on every keyboard.
  * The app id is now io.github.gearbench.Gearbench and the GNOME Shell
    extension gearbench-focus@gearbench.github.io, after the gearbench
    organization on GitHub, which now holds the repository. After the
    upgrade, set Gearbench's switch in Settings > Notifications again if you
    had changed it, pin Gearbench to the dock again, log out and back in
    once, and then turn the extension on with the window's Turn on. Until it
    runs, game profiles follow only X11 windows.
  * Spatial sound plays to one output for every headset, Gearbench Spatial
    (gearbench-spatial), in place of Astro A50 Spatial. Change each game's
    launch option to PULSE_SINK=gearbench-spatial %command%: a game still on
    gearbench-a50-spatial plays on the default output in stereo. If spatial
    sound was on, switch it off and on once after the update.
  * config.json and the game profiles' files are now version 3: they keep
    the lighting for each keyboard model, config.json its brightness too,
    and the button bindings for each mouse model. Settings from an earlier
    Gearbench load as the G915's and the G502 X PLUS's. Before each file's
    first save, a copy goes to backup/ in the settings folder; an earlier
    Gearbench does not read the new files, and copying the backups back
    returns to it.
  * Among the fixes: DPI notices no longer stop GNOME Shell from showing any
    app's notifications until the next login, and a G432 adapter that
    answers its firmware read short no longer stops the service. Security
    fixes: the daemon refuses a runtime folder another account could swap,
    and the G432's equalizer, noise suppression and spatial sound no longer
    take a sandboxed app's copy of the headset's output or microphone.

